The one ray of hope in that regard I've seen in mainstream 3D games is 
the increased emphasis on physics engines lately. Physics engines are 
neat because they go beyond eye candy (though they provide that too) and 
really do offer new gameplay possibilities. Half-Life 2 was the first 
game I played that had a really good one. Unfortunatly they wasted it by 
making the game so linier that the only time you really got to use 
physics in a "clever" way was in obviously set-up puzzles that could 
have been scripted without the aid of a physic engine anyway.

